1,2 $ Growing with Cistus, Halimium or Tuberaria
1,6 $ Growing with Helianthemum
1,0 $$
1,0 $ Retour à la clé principale $$f"Hebeloma_Henry_Beker_2016.cle"

2,0 $ Cortina present (easily visible when young and sometimes persistent), spores ellipsoid-ovoid and indextrinoid $ H. cistophilum
2,3 $ Without cortina, spores amygdaloid and at least slightly dextrinoid

3,0 $ Spores distinctly ornamented (O3) and weakly but distinctly dextrinoid. Pileus not pruinose $ H. cavipes
3,4 $ Spores almost smooth to weakly ornamented (at most 02) and strongly dextrinoid. Pileus sometimes pruinose

4,0 $ Average spore width <= 5.5 µm $ H. alboerumpens
4,5 $ Average spore width > 5.5 µm

5,0 $ Average cheilocystidia apex > 7 µm and spores very strongly dextrinoid, becoming dark brick to dark reddish-brown $ H. plesiocistum
5,0 $ Average cheilocystidia apex < 7 µm and spores strongly dextrinoid, becoming yellow brown to orange-brown or brick $ H. erumpens

6,0 $ Cheilocystidia primarily cylindrical $ H. laterinum
6,0 $ Cheilocytsidia primarily ventricose $ H. sinapizans
6,7 $ Cheilocystidia swollen at apex, average cheilocystium ratio A/B >= 1.30

7,0 $ Many spores distinctly ornamented (O3), most cheilocystidia clavate-ventricose $ H. cavipes
7,8 $ Most spores at most weakly ornamented (O2), cheilocystidia clavate-ventricose or gently clavate

8,0 $ Most cheilocystidia gently clavate and average length > 40 µm $ H. velutipes
8,9 $ Most cheilocystidia clavate-ventricose and average length <= 40 µm

9,0 $ Average spore width > 5.5 µm and average spore length > 10.5 µm $ H. griseopruinatum
9,0 $ Average spore width < 5.5 µm and average spore length <= 10.5 µm $ H. theobrominum
